--TEST--
a `...` call argument supplies a `...$rest` tail and nothing else: every fixed parameter is still written out, and the tail's element type is what the subject's `array<T>` is checked against (`rule:types/arrays`, `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Join {
    public static function of(string $sep, string ...$parts): string {
        return $sep;
    }
    public static function pair(string $a, string $b): string {
        return $a;
    }
}
array<string> $parts = ["a", "b"];
array<int> $nums = [1, 2];
// A spread lands wholly in the variadic tail, so `$sep` still needs one:
// how many entries `$parts` holds is a run-time fact.
echo Join::of(...$parts), "\n";
// There is no tail at all here.
echo Join::pair(...$parts), "\n";
// The tail's element type is the expectation, one level up — so the refusal
// names both array types rather than the subject alone.
echo Join::of("-", ...$nums), "\n";
// The same rule at a `Core` member: `Core\Path::join(string, string ...)`.
echo Core\Path::join("a", ...$nums), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0489]: a `...` argument has no variadic parameter to spread into
   --> case.nvs:14:15
